# orb-transit.js

File: src/data/presets/orb-transit.js (generated: never edited by hand)

From the dataset: orb-transit — Transits of Venus, 1761, 1769 and 2012: every contact timing in
  Newcomb's tabular summary (1891) and the adopted positions of the stations, our transcription, and
  the contact III timings of the venus2012.de project, reduced into the astronomical unit with DE440
  (data/orb/transits/, every input with its own .explain)
Inputs:
- data/orb/transits/transcription/*.csv — transcription
- data/orb/transits/v2012/daten3.txt — download, not in the public repository (hash and source in
  its .explain)
Collected: 2026-11-18
Licence: other — Newcomb 1891 (US government publication, public domain); the venus2012.de timings
  were published openly by the project for evaluation (archived copy, cited); JPL DE440, public.
Sources:
- S. Newcomb, Discussion of observations of the transits of Venus in 1761 and 1769, Astronomical
  Papers prepared for the use of the American Ephemeris and Nautical Almanac, vol. II, part V
  (Washington, 1891), chapter IV (table of adopted positions, pp. 343-345) and chapter VI (tabular
  summary, pp. 364-371) <https://archive.org/details/02aAstronomicalPapersPreparedForTheUse>
- U. Backhaus (Universität Duisburg-Essen), Transit of Venus 2012, project "Contact times"
  (www.venus2012.de): the contact III timings sent by participants, file participants/daten3.txt as
  archived by the Internet Archive on 2024-05-19
  <http://web.archive.org/web/20240519220217/http://www.venus2012.de/participants/daten3.txt>
- R. S. Park, W. M. Folkner, J. G. Williams and D. H. Boggs, The JPL Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides
  DE440 and DE441, Astron. J. 161 (2021) 105
  <https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/spk/planets/>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- TRANSIT_FIT.a: the baseline between stations is in Earth radii (Newcomb's log ρ sin φ′, log ρ
  cos φ′); the unit comes out in Earth radii and is turned into km with a⊕ = 6378.137 km
- TRANSIT_ROWS, TRANSIT_FIT: JPL DE440 gives the shape of the orbits in astronomical units
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Contact times that do not depend on the station's
  place on the Earth the way a distant Sun requires, or that no single unit fits across the
  stations.
